From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:05:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf: halve the CPU cost of the list walk and the GL renderer
 per draw

Measured with ARM instruction counts (a Cortex-A9 build of ge_replay under QEMU,
ten gameplay frames of Infected): the renderer's own work per frame went from
6.2 to 3.0 million instructions and the list walk's from 6.0 to 3.5 million.
On the Vita the frame was CPU-bound on the GE thread (walk 21 ms, submit 36 ms).

List walk (GeState):
- the command loop keeps its per-word checks to a subtraction and a compare;
  the list trace is read on the slow path only, and handle_command() is
  inlined into it; matrix uploads, BASE, VADDR, IADDR, VTYPE are handled in
  the loop
- register traces and block transfers moved out of the hot handler
- lighting and material versions move only when a value changes (a game sends
  the same values per model): fewer renderer refreshes and 65 more merged
  draws per frame; a new matrix_version does the same for world/view/proj
- ignored commands are counted in an array, not a map
- decoded vertices use an allocator that does not construct on resize()
- vertex placement memoised per vertex type; 16-bit indices copied, ranged
  and rebased in vectorisable loops; NEON skinning multiplies by lane (scalar
  operands went through the stack) and skips zero weights before converting
- the split renderer takes a draw's vertex and index storage instead of
  copying it (Renderer::submit_taking)

GL renderer:
- inline word compares instead of memcmp; DrawState reordered (texture first)
  and compared only up to the matrices, which follow from a transform serial
- uniform uploads templated on the uniform; lighting and matrices compared
  only when they changed since the program last had them
- the matrix memo keyed by matrix_version; lround inlined
- direct-mapped front caches for palette hashes, textures, samplers and
  programs (no hash/modulo per draw)
- triangle strips converted two triangles at a time, lists in 16-bit lanes
- batches are appended straight into the frame's GPU scratch memory on the
  Vita (BatchBuffer): no staging copy at the flush
- merge-break statistics only with ge_replay --stats

Verified: 25 replayed gameplay frames pixel-identical to the previous build
(direct and --threaded); a live mission-1 run, threaded and split, gives the
same screenshots at four points as the build before; ctest 42/42; the NEON
skinning matched the scalar decoder over 100,000 runs (QEMU). The Vulkan
renderer was only syntax-checked. Not yet run on a Vita.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

